WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.710
This is a really great question because
everyone wants to know how we might you

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:15.990
know improve regenerative outcomes and
human patience right and so one way that

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.050
you can really start to think about how
to do that is to ask the question what

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.430
are the genes that enable axolotls to
regenerate so well? and if you have the

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.759
whole genome then you can consider all
the genes because you know you have the

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:29.730
identity of all the genes and so what we
can do is we can say what are the genes

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:33.780
that help Axolotl's are necessary for Axolotl's to regenerate and then we can

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.760
compare those genes to the genes in the
human and we can say okay do humans have

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.359
these genes or do they not have these
genes and for ones the humans might not

00:00:42.359 --> 00:00:47.429
have we can say what would happen if you
gave for instance a mammal that gene for

00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:50.999
instance what would be the consequences
could you at some level improve

00:00:50.999 --> 00:00:54.780
regeneration maybe even just a little
bit and then another thing you can do is

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.760
say okay what about for the genes that
both axolotl's and humans and mice all of

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.220
them have are they a hundred percent
identical you know do they make the

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.540
exact same protein or not and do all the
same cells make that kind of protein etc

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.689
so you can go in and actually compare
you know point by point to see exactly

00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:16.259
how similar or different they are and
that will give you some clues about you

00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:19.890
know where they might where they might
be different and if they're important

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:24.000
for regeneration you could say well
maybe the human one is perhaps broken in

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.810
this spot right so that would be really
important. It's also really important

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.310
experimentally for us to be able to
assign function to particular genes. The

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:36.750
gold standard in genetics is to remove
them so to delete the gene and then see

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.030
what the consequences on the thing
you're studying so in this case

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:44.250
regeneration and with the technologies
that we have now so for instance CRISPR

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.729
editing technologies you can do that if
you know what gene you're studying but

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:51.119
if you don't know what all the other
genes are that the axolotl has you might

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:54.990
secretly be mucking with one of the
other ones or many of the other ones and

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:58.979
so having the whole genome allows us to
much better predict what might be the

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:04.439
accidental you know what we call off
target genes that we might also be

00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:07.259
hitting so that we can be much more
specific about saying okay we are

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:10.289
studying this one particular gene and
this is its function rather than not

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.750
knowing that we're really disrupting a
whole bunch of different ones at the

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.230
same time.

